sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/turing-capable-network-topologies--grok-4-7--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Exact n=3 sign-pattern census by interval bounds" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/turing-capable-network-topologies> ;
    dct:description "Enumerate all 3^9 = 19683 sign patterns in {-1, 0, +1}^{3×3}. Quotient by simultaneous permutation of the three species only after the raw census, so the quotient cannot hide a disagreement. A pattern is Turing-capable if there exist positive rate magnitudes and a non-negative diffusion vector, not a scalar multiple of the identity, such that J is Hurwitz and J minus q squared D is not Hurwitz for some q > 0. Hurwitz for 3×3 is the three Routh–Hurwitz inequalities on the characteristic polynomial. Search by log-uniform samples of the nonzero magnitudes in [10^{-2}, 10^{2}], 200 draws per pattern, and a log grid of q. Every pattern not decided by a sample goes to interval branch-and-bound on the log-magnitudes. Capable means a concrete witness box. Incapable means the parameter domain is covered by boxes that violate the conditions. A pattern still unresolved below a minimum box width is a failure of the census, not a silent negative. Compare witnesses with the Turing-instability labels in Marcon et al. 2016 and Scholes et al. 2019, not with their broader patterning labels." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A complete partition of the 19683 patterns into capable and incapable, with a witness or a covering certificate for each, and an empty disagreement list against the published Turing labels or an explicit list of disagreements." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: More than 1 percent of patterns remain unresolved, or a published Turing-capable network is certified incapable, or the converse. Either is a failed census or a failed atlas entry, and the approach must say which." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/computation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Enumerate the 19683 sign patterns and the diffusion supports. 2. Apply the Routh–Hurwitz test to samples and to the q grid. 3. Branch-and-bound every undecided pattern with interval arithmetic. 4. Emit a witness or a covering certificate for each pattern. 5. Match the capable set against the Turing labels in the two atlases and list every disagreement." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): hard" ;
    skos:note "Tool: enumeration" ;
    skos:note "Tool: sign-pattern-matrices" ;
    skos:note "Tool: linear-algebra" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/turing-ddi> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/turing-wavelength> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/turing-morphogen-hypothesis> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.14022> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cels.2019.07.007> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/turing-capable-network-topologies> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Classify the network topologies that admit diffusion-driven instability" .


  <https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.14022> dct:title "High-throughput mathematical analysis identifies Turing networks for patterning with equally diffusing signals" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Marcon et al. 2016, atlas used only for its Turing-instability labels" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cels.2019.07.007> dct:title "A Comprehensive Network Atlas Reveals That Turing Patterns Are Common but Not Robust" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Scholes et al. 2019, network atlas of Turing patterns" .
}

